  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is a distance MBA worth it in India? Mostly yes for working professionals who need a
    recognised credential without leaving their job, and mostly no for freshers hoping it replaces
    a campus placement process it was never built to provide.</strong>
    A distance or online MBA is asynchronous, remote, open to a much broader audience than an
    executive MBA, and regulated separately from a full-time program. Whether it is worth it
    depends almost entirely on what specific problem you are asking it to solve — and whether it
    is one credential inside a wider, high-income skill portfolio, or the only thing you are
    counting on to move your income forward.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A distance/online MBA is worth it mainly for people already employed who need a recognised credential for a promotion policy, HR eligibility rule, or structured business knowledge, without pausing income.</li>
      <li>Always verify UGC-DEB entitlement for the exact university and exact program on the current academic session's list at deb.ugc.ac.in before paying a fee — approval is program-specific and changes yearly.</li>
      <li>Fees run roughly Rs 55,000 at public universities like IGNOU up to Rs 2-3.3 lakh at online-only private universities like Amity — a fraction of a full-time MBA's cost, but with no campus placement drive attached.</li>
